explanation:
The library's `geometricMean a b` is defined as `conjSqrt a (sqrt (conjSqrt a⁻¹ʳ b))`, i.e. a^{1/2}(a^{-1/2} b a^{-1/2})^{1/2} a^{1/2}. Taking a = S₁⁻¹ gives S₁^{-1/2}(S₁^{1/2} S₂ S₁^{1/2})^{1/2} S₁^{-1/2}. This is exactly the optimal linear transport matrix from covariance S₁ to covariance S₂: it is the unique positive semidefinite A with A S₁ A = S₂.

Displacement interpolation follows x ↦ (1−t)x + tT(x), so its linear part is (1−t)I + tA, which is what the definition says. The module's lemmas agree with this reading:
- B₀ = I;
- B₁ = A;
- B₁ S₁ B₁ᵀ = S₂ when S₁ is positive definite and S₂ is positive semidefinite;
- pushing forward along the interpolation map gives the Gaussian with covariance B_t S₁ B_tᵀ.

The definition gives junk values when S₁ is not positive definite (the ring inverse and the conjSqrt conventions take over). This is acceptable, since the notion only makes sense for positive definite S₁.

Real matrices over a finite index type are the natural setting for Gaussian optimal transport. Mathlib has no counterpart.
